\description{
Builds the \code{targets} pipeline steps for a high-dimensional WGCNA (hdWGCNA)
co-expression network analysis, following the hdWGCNA basic + differential
module-eigengene tutorials. A single shared metacell-preparation step
(\code{wgcna_prep}) is created once, and the per-group steps below are created for
every element of \code{group} (target names carry the group, e.g. \code{wgcna_mait}):
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{wgcna_prep} — RNA assay prep (join layers, normalise, variable features,
scale) then \code{SetupForWGCNA} + \code{MetacellsByGroups} and the metacell embedding.
\item \verb{wgcna_<group>_powertest} — \code{SetDatExpr} for the group + \code{TestSoftPowers},
returning the scale-free fit table and the chosen power rather than the
Seurat object.
\item \verb{wgcna_<group>_soft_power} — the soft power chosen exactly as
\code{hdWGCNA::PlotSoftPowers} highlights it (lowest tested power with
\code{SFT.R.sq >= sft_rsquared}).
\item \verb{wgcna_<group>} — \code{ConstructNetwork} (at the selected soft power) +
\code{ModuleEigengenes} + \code{ModuleConnectivity} + \code{ResetModuleNames}, and, when
\code{trait_col} is given, \code{ModuleTraitCorrelation} of the modules against one-hot
indicators of each \code{trait_groups} level. The correlation is computed once
across all of the group's cells (a single constant \code{group.by}), so the stored
result has only an \code{"all_cells"} entry and is not split by the object's
cluster identities.
\item \verb{wgcna_<group>_dmes} — differential module eigengenes (\code{FindDMEs}) for every
pairwise comparison of \code{trait_groups} within the group (only created when
\code{trait_col} and \code{trait_groups} are supplied).
\item \verb{wgcna_<group>_enrichr} — module gene-set enrichment via \code{RunEnrichr}, run
once per \code{enrichr_dbs} entry and row-bound into a single data frame (each row
tagged with its \code{db}); created when \code{run_enrichr = TRUE}.
}

All values passed to the hdWGCNA / WGCNA functions are exposed as arguments.
Every step that uses WGCNA multithreading or loads the whole shared Seurat
object is \strong{pinned to \code{main}} and is \emph{not} affected by \code{deployment} /
\code{controller}: \code{wgcna_prep}, the power test, the soft-power pick, the network
and the Enrichr step. WGCNA's threading is incompatible with \code{crew} workers,
and the power test / network hold a full copy of the object. Only the
differential-module-eigengene step (\verb{wgcna_<group>_dmes}, a Wilcoxon test)
honours \code{deployment} and \code{controller}, so it can be sent to a worker (and to a
named controller when a controller group is used). \code{tom_name} is set to the
group label.
}
\section{Caching the prepared object}{


\code{wgcna_prep} is the memory peak of this factory, and by a wide margin. It
holds the whole Seurat object, adds a normalised assay layer and a dense
\code{scale.data} over the variable features, and only then reduces to metacells.
On a dataset of ~50k cells that is comfortably into double-digit GB, which is
more than a small server or a memory-capped container has, while the steps
below it work on metacells and are far cheaper.

\code{prep_path} exists so that peak has to be paid once, on a machine that can
afford it, rather than on every machine that wants the results. When it is
set, \code{wgcna_prep} becomes a \code{format = "file"} target that:
\enumerate{
\item checks the file with \code{\link[=hdwgcna_prep_is_valid]{hdwgcna_prep_is_valid()}} and, if it passes, returns
the path without building anything;
\item otherwise runs the preparation, writes it with \code{\link[=save_seurat]{save_seurat()}} and returns
the path.
}

So the usual workflow is to run the pipeline once where there is memory, copy
the resulting file across with the rest of \verb{out/}, and run everything else
anywhere. \strong{The file wins over the pipeline}: an upstream change does not
refresh it, exactly so that a machine which cannot rebuild it does not try.
Delete the file to force a rebuild.

The object is a plain Seurat object, so it can also be built outside the
pipeline. This is what the target does, and a hand-made file only has to
match it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(Seurat); library(hdWGCNA); library(WGCNA)
# load the seurat object and basic setup
obj <- scitargets::as_seurat("out/seurat/seurat_obj.qs2")
DefaultAssay(obj) <- "RNA"
obj[["RNA"]] <- SeuratObject::JoinLayers(obj[["RNA"]])
obj <- NormalizeData(obj, verbose = FALSE)
obj <- FindVariableFeatures(obj, verbose = FALSE)
obj <- ScaleData(obj, features = VariableFeatures(obj), verbose = FALSE)

# WGCNA setup (see https://smorabit.github.io/hdWGCNA/articles/basic_tutorial.html#set-up-seurat-object-for-wgcna)
obj <- SetupForWGCNA(obj,
 gene_select = "fraction",     # gene_select
  fraction = 0.05,             # fraction
  wgcna_name = "hdwgcna"       # wgcna_name
  )
obj <- MetacellsByGroups(obj,
  group.by    = c("predicted.celltype.l2", "patient_id"), # clustering_col and patient_col
  ident.group = "predicted.celltype.l2",                  # metacell_group_by
  reduction   = "harmony",                                # reduction
   k = 25,                                                # metacell_k
   max_shared = 10                                        # metacell_max_shared
   )
obj <- NormalizeMetacells(obj)
obj <- ScaleMetacells(obj, features = VariableFeatures(obj))
obj <- RunPCAMetacells(obj, features = VariableFeatures(obj))
obj <- RunHarmonyMetacells(
   obj,
   group.by.vars = "patient_id"      # patient_cols
   )
obj <- RunUMAPMetacells(obj,
   reduction = "harmony",            # reduction
   dims = 1:15                       # metacells_dims
   )
scitargets::save_seurat(obj, "./out/seurat/wgcna_prep.qs2")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Every argument above must match the \code{tar_hdwgcna()} call that will read the
file (\code{assay}, \code{gene_select}, \code{fraction}, \code{wgcna_name}, \code{metacell_group_by},
\code{clustering_col}, \code{reduction}, \code{metacell_k}, \code{metacell_max_shared},
\code{metacell_dims}, \code{patient_col}), because none of them is re-checked: only the
structural conditions in \code{\link[=hdwgcna_prep_is_valid]{hdwgcna_prep_is_valid()}} are. A file prepared with
a different \code{fraction} is accepted and silently changes the gene set the
networks are built on.

Set \code{prep_path = NULL} to restore the previous behaviour, where the prepared
object is an ordinary target kept in the \code{targets} store.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
list(
  scitargets::tar_hdwgcna(
    group = "MAIT",
    input_obj = "seurat_merged_filtered",
    clustering_col = "predicted.celltype.l2",
    patient_col = "patient_id",
    trait_col = "cohort_clinic_preterrah",
    trait_groups = c("CR1M/6M", "IR6M", "NR1M")
  )
)
}
}
